entry.content:
&lt;p&gt;watch&lt;br /&gt;
this acorn drop,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;it cracks, splintered,&lt;br /&gt;
against the august pavement&lt;br /&gt;
burnt black by the harsh&lt;br /&gt;
southern sun, summers since&lt;br /&gt;
past until cracked and&lt;br /&gt;
hardened oil paved the path&lt;br /&gt;
where older acorns&lt;br /&gt;
fell and grew.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;but the crow stares down,&lt;br /&gt;
its dark eyes indifferent&lt;br /&gt;
as an engine roars below&lt;br /&gt;
and the acorn&lt;br /&gt;
smashs to a pulp,&lt;br /&gt;
laid bare,&lt;br /&gt;
exposed to the crow&lt;br /&gt;
which its ancestors&lt;br /&gt;
long ago conquered.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;a heavy blanket never slept&lt;br /&gt;
unto the tip of night I wept&lt;br /&gt;
i climbed upon the blackthorn tree&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;em&gt;and cut a sweeter apple for thee&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;i have seen the best minds of my generation cowering in coffeehouses,&lt;br /&gt;
    maddened by cold brew, frantically breathing, while they still can.&lt;br /&gt;
ecstatic typists lay around us, broken, endangered, barely aware,&lt;br /&gt;
who trade ginger and sell roots while urban structures still stand&lt;br /&gt;
    buying focus and forgetfulness,&lt;br /&gt;
    abstract freedom found in screens, owned by corpses of collectives and corporations&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;whose plugged in spirits burst and bubble with memory mappings and pointers to a void,&lt;br /&gt;
who work faster now, screaming in a quiet, numb, neutered sort of way&lt;br /&gt;
    harmonizing with the other strung up souls,&lt;br /&gt;
who revel in mechanical sanctity, divested of their own,&lt;br /&gt;
    dogs for the towers which cynically shelter them,&lt;br /&gt;
    laying bricks for the great mechanical edifice:&lt;br /&gt;
    a bridge to cross the rubicon.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;who quench their fears with homegrown needles, hoping to hit arteries.&lt;br /&gt;
who forget secrets each day, meeting again with each blackened sip and retold open lie.&lt;br /&gt;
who hide themselves in art, in its promises and doubts.&lt;br /&gt;
who cling to shreds of liberalism, once discarded for red-black wet dreams&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;whose paranoid fits leave them backwards on chairs, facing broken cameras and torn up curtains.&lt;br /&gt;
who worship under the blinding, burning light, smoldering in chiaroscuro.&lt;br /&gt;
who chew on bitter chalk, dry up, cease skinning thoughts, voice ripping fantasies.&lt;br /&gt;
who push it down their throats, trying to relish in the salt&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;who crave that queer, blessed unrest&lt;br /&gt;
    that humming, buzzing air that keeps them marching&lt;br /&gt;
    and makes them more alive than the others&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;whose agony and terror forced them through splintering wooden doors, into the cafe lights.&lt;br /&gt;
who see one another by curse-marks on foreheads, hands, and necks.&lt;br /&gt;
who yearn for her unclean, kosher body—born broken, piercing, demonettes—and can&#39;t bear it&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;who find themselves as failed men, deluded and reshaped.&lt;br /&gt;
who lean closer, weakening at the sight of her thin, nimble hands&lt;br /&gt;
    grazing their tatooed freckles, crumbled by its emerging eyespots.&lt;br /&gt;
who whisper ballads, two-part soliloquies of ecstasy through hours lived and slept&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;who walk onto grass, away from the light&lt;br /&gt;
    finding the deepest darkness between buildings&lt;br /&gt;
who arch their backs, hold the other closer,&lt;br /&gt;
    shrieking and moaning&lt;br /&gt;
    pierced by silver and peachwood&lt;br /&gt;
    squeezing chitin and porcelain&lt;br /&gt;
    rend the flesh from their skin&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;who twirl and pretend, gaze longingly, skating down cobbled streets&lt;br /&gt;
    to find themselves at train stops,&lt;br /&gt;
    with dreams of wildflowers and rusted boats&lt;/p&gt;
